== Counting and results ==
Following the election on 9 July 2014, Joko Widodo announced his victory based on [[quick count]]s of votes from several zones; most of these independent pollsters indicated a Joko Widodo victory (52–53% of votes to Prabowo's 46–48%).<ref>{{cite news |url=https://edition.cnn.com/2014/07/10/world/asia/indonesia-candidates-wait/ |publisher=CNN |title=Indonesian presidential candidate declares victory, opponent says not so fast |date=7 July 2014 |access-date=22 July 2014 |author1=Madison Park |author2=Kathy Quiano |archive-date=14 July 2014 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140714190609/http://edition.cnn.com/2014/07/10/world/asia/indonesia-candidates-wait/}}</ref> Prabowo also claimed victory, citing other polls.<ref name="ST1">{{cite news |title=Indonesia polls: Prabowo claims win although quick count favours Jokowi |url=http://www.straitstimes.com/news/asia/south-east-asia/story/indonesia-polls-prabowo-claims-win-although-quick-count-favours-joko |work=The Straits Times |location=Singapore |author=Zubaidah Nazeer |date=9 July 2014 |archive-date=9 July 2014 |access-date=22 July 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140709175919/http://www.straitstimes.com/news/asia/south-east-asia/story/indonesia-polls-prabowo-claims-win-although-quick-count-favours-joko |url-status=dead}}</ref> As the official count continued, the KPU released scans of the tally (C1) forms from each polling station on its official website, allowing downloads of the official data.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.thejakartapost.com/news/2014/07/22/jokowi-praises-prabowo.html |title=Jokowi praises Prabowo |work=The Jakarta Post |access-date=23 July 2014 |archive-date=23 July 2014 |date=22 July 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140723210222/http://www.thejakartapost.com/news/2014/07/22/jokowi-praises-prabowo.html |url-status=dead}}</ref>

In the lead up to the official announcement of the official results by the KPU, Prabowo pushed for the Commission to delay the announcement by two weeks, allowing his party to investigate claimed manipulations of the voting process. This request was denied.<ref name="CNN"/> The Prabowo camp also called for a new vote in some zones.<ref name="Bloomberg"/> However, several Prabowo supporters congratulated Joko Widodo on his election or conceded the election. PAN politician Hanafi Rais, writing three days before the results were announced, sent a press release which stated: "We congratulate Bapak Joko Widodo and Jusuf Kalla — who will helm the national leadership for the next five years".<ref name="JG1">{{cite web |url=http://www.thejakartaglobe.com/news/jokowi-confirmed-indonesias-next-president-but-still-no-surrender-from-desperate-prabowo/ |title=Jokowi Confirmed Indonesia's Next President, But Still No Surrender From Desperate Prabowo |date=22 July 2014 |access-date=22 July 2014 |work=Jakarta Globe |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140725095019/http://www.thejakartaglobe.com/news/jokowi-confirmed-indonesias-next-president-but-still-no-surrender-from-desperate-prabowo/ |archive-date=25 July 2014 |url-status=dead}}</ref> The same day, Prabowo's campaign manager [[Mahfud MD]] returned his mandate to Prabowo, stating that the election was over;<ref name="JG1"/> he was replaced by Lt. Gen. [[Yunus Yosfiah]] on 22 July 2014.<ref name="Mahfud">{{cite web |url=http://www.thejakartapost.com/news/2014/07/22/mahfud-replaced-presidential-race-takes-surprise-turn.html |title=Mahfud replaced as presidential race takes surprise turn |date=22 July 2014 |access-date=22 July 2014 |work=The Jakarta Post |archive-date=23 July 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140723210227/http://www.thejakartapost.com/news/2014/07/22/mahfud-replaced-presidential-race-takes-surprise-turn.html |url-status=dead}}</ref>

[[File:Jokowi voting.jpg|thumb|right|alt=[[Joko Widodo]] votes in Indonesia's 2014 presidential election|[[Joko Widodo]] votes in Indonesia's 2014 presidential election]]

Out of fear that inter-party tension could lead to riots such as [[May 1998 riots of Indonesia|those]] which led to the downfall of former president [[Suharto]], the Indonesian government deployed over 250,000 police officers throughout the country. In [[central Jakarta]], hundreds of police were stationed – particularly around the KPU's offices.<ref name="ABCAus"/> Following bomb threats against Jakarta City Hall, after the KPU's announcement military officials tightened security around it Commission's headquarters.<ref name="Mulyono">{{cite news |url=http://www.thejakartapost.com/news/2014/07/22/threat-leads-jakarta-military-commander-close-jokowi.html |title=Threat leads Jakarta military commander to close in on Jokowi |author=Hans Nicholas Jong |work=The Jakarta Post |date=22 July 2014 |archive-date=23 July 2014 |access-date=23 July 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140723210232/http://www.thejakartapost.com/news/2014/07/22/threat-leads-jakarta-military-commander-close-jokowi.html |url-status=dead}}</ref> A group of Prabowo supporters staged a non-violent protest near the offices.<ref name="ABCAus">{{cite news |url=http://www.abc.net.au/news/2014-07-22/indonesia-election-winner-announced/5616014 |title=Indonesia elections: Joko 'Jokowi' Widodo confirmed as next president after official results released |author=Greg Jenett |publisher=Australian Broadcasting Corporation |date=22 July 2014 |archive-date=22 July 2014 |access-date=22 July 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140722183856/http://www.abc.net.au/news/2014-07-22/indonesia-election-winner-announced/5616014 |url-status=dead}}</ref>

===Prabowo's withdrawal===
On 22 July 2014, the day that the KPU was due to announce its official tally, Prabowo withdrew from the recapitulation process after having insisted on his victory since the initial quick counts were released. He attributed this withdrawal to Indonesia "failing in its duty to democracy" because of "massive cheating that is structured and systematic",<ref name="SMH1">{{Cite news |url=https://www.smh.com.au/world/prabowo-subianto-withdraws-from-indonesian-presidential-election-on-day-vote-was-to-be-declared-20140722-zvte5.html |title=Prabowo Subianto 'withdraws' from Indonesian presidential election on day vote was to be declared |work=Sidney Morning Herald |date=22 July 2014 |access-date=22 July 2014 |author-link1=Michael Bachelard |last=Bachelard |first=Michael}}</ref> and stated that he and Hatta "exercise our constitutional right to reject the presidential election and declare it unconstitutional".<ref name="CNN">{{Cite news |url=https://edition.cnn.com/2014/07/21/world/asia/indonesia-election-result/ |title=Prabowo withdraws from Indonesian election process |publisher=CNN |date=22 July 2014 |access-date=22 July 2014 |author1=Kathy Quiano |author2=Madison Park |author3=Casey Tolan}}</ref> His speech, aired live, implied that he would challenge the results in the Constitutional Court (''Mahkamah Konstitusi'').<ref name="SMH1"/> Later reports indicated confusion over whether Prabowo had resigned from the election or simply rejected the count.<ref name="Bloomberg"/>

According to Douglas Ramage, Managing Director for Indonesia at BowerGroupAsia,<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.bowergroupasia.com/The-Team.aspx#Ramage |title=Bower Group Asia - The Team |access-date=30 November 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141130023731/http://bowergroupasia.com/The-Team.aspx#Ramage |archive-date=30 November 2014 |url-status=dead}}</ref> this was the first time since ''[[Post-Suharto era|reformasi]] (the Reformation)'' began in 1998 that the legitimacy of the election process has been questioned; he declared that the country was entering "uncharted territory".<ref name="CNN"/> The legality of a Prabowo challenge is questionable, as – if he withdrew – he is no longer considered a presidential candidate.<ref name="CNN"/> If he can make the challenge, according to ''[[The Jakarta Post]]'', the gap between the two candidates is sufficient to make such a challenge difficult.<ref name="The Jakarta Post-2014">{{Cite news |url=http://www.thejakartapost.com/news/2014/07/22/official-final-tally-jokowi-5315-prabowo-4685.html |title=Official, final tally: Jokowi 53.15%, Prabowo 46.85% |work=The Jakarta Post |date=22 July 2014 |access-date=22 July 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140724170403/http://www.thejakartapost.com/news/2014/07/22/official-final-tally-jokowi-5315-prabowo-4685.html |archive-date=24 July 2014 |url-status=dead}}</ref> Under the presidential election law, Prabowo could face up to six years in prison and a 100 billion rupiah ($10 million) fine for withdrawing.<ref name="CNN"/><ref name="SMH1"/>
 
Following the announcement, the value of the [[Indonesian rupiah]] dropped by 0.3%, and the [[JSX Composite]] fell by 0.9%.<ref name="Bloomberg"/> Observers denied Prabowo's allegations of cheating, finding that the elections were "generally fair and free"; Maswadi Rauf of the [[University of Indonesia]] stated that there was "no sign of significant fraud", and that Prabowo's withdrawal simply reflected "the real attitudes of the elite, who are not yet ready to accept losing".<ref name="ABC1">{{cite news |title=Jakarta Governor Wins Indonesian Presidency |url=https://abcnews.go.com/International/wireStory/widodo-leads-indonesia-set-declare-president-24658389 |access-date=22 July 2014 |date=22 June 2014 |archive-date=25 July 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140725104552/http://abcnews.go.com/International/wireStory/widodo-leads-indonesia-set-declare-president-24658389 |work=ABC News |author1=Niniek Karmini |author2=Ali Kotarumalos |url-status=dead}}</ref>

===Announcement and reaction===
After Prabowo's withdrawal, his witnesses also left the announcement ceremony. However, the official tally continued; the Commission chief, Husni Kamil Manik, said that they had already fulfilled their obligations by inviting the witnesses.<ref name="Tempo1"/> A victory for Joko Widodo was expected,<ref name="Bloomberg">{{cite news |url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/2014-07-21/indonesia-election-results-due-as-widodo-leads-unofficial-counts.html |title=Widodo Heads for Indonesia Win as Prabowo Withdraws From Count |author1=Novrida Manurung |author2=Rieka Rahadiana |author3=Yoga Rusmana |date=22 July 2014 |access-date=22 July 2014 |work=Bloomberg}}</ref> and realised hours later, although the initially planned 4:00&nbsp;p.m. announcement was delayed for four hours.<ref name="Tempo1">{{cite news |title=KPU Postpones Election Results Announcement |work=Tempo |url=http://en.tempo.co/read/news/2014/07/22/055595012/KPU-Postpones-Election-Results-Announcement |author=Yolanda Ryan Armindya |date=22 July 2014 |archive-date=1 November 2014 |access-date=22 July 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141101081325/http://en.tempo.co/read/news/2014/07/22/055595012/KPU-Postpones-Election-Results-Announcement |url-status=dead}}</ref> The KPU gave Joko Widodo a victory of 53.15% of the vote (representing 70.99 million voters), to Prabowo's 46.85% (62.57 million votes).<ref name="The Jakarta Post-2014"/> This was the closest vote in the history of free elections in the country; the two previous elections, in 2004 and 2009, had been landslide victories for Yudhoyono.<ref name="Reuters">{{cite news |title=Indonesian president-elect Jokowi calls for unity after bitter election |author1=Jonathan Thatcher |author2=Kanupriya Kapoor |url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-indonesia-election-idUSKBN0FQ23F20140722 |access-date=23 July 2014 |date=22 June 2014 |work=Reuters |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140722081543/https://www.reuters.com/article/2014/07/22/us-indonesia-election-idUSKBN0FQ23F20140722 |archive-date=22 July 2014}}</ref>

The Prabowo camp continued to reject the KPU's count, announcing that they trusted the count provided by the PKS, which gave a Prabowo victory, more than the Commission's.<ref>{{cite news |title=Prabowo camp says PKS tally more accurate than KPU's |url=http://www.thejakartapost.com/news/2014/07/22/prabowo-camp-says-pks-tally-more-accurate-kpus.html |access-date=22 July 2014 |date=22 June 2014 |archive-date=23 July 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140723232210/http://www.thejakartapost.com/news/2014/07/22/prabowo-camp-says-pks-tally-more-accurate-kpus.html |work=The Jakarta Post |url-status=dead}}</ref> Prabowo's camp later stated that it intended to report the KPU to the police for continuing its recapitulation despite calls for a delay and questions of the vote's validity.<ref>{{cite news |title=Indonesian Stocks, Rupiah Slump as Prabowo Contests Vote |author1=Yudith Ho |author2=Harry Suhartono |url=http://www.businessweek.com/news/2014-07-22/indonesian-stocks-rupiah-slump-after-prabowo-rejects-election |access-date=23 July 2014 |date=22 June 2014 |work=Business Week |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140723031528/http://www.businessweek.com/news/2014-07-22/indonesian-stocks-rupiah-slump-after-prabowo-rejects-election |archive-date=23 July 2014}}</ref>

After the announcement, Joko Widodo stated that growing up under the authoritarian and corrupt [[New Order (Indonesia)|New Order]], he would have never expected someone with a lower-class background to become president. ''[[The New York Times]]'' reported him as saying, "now, it's quite similar to America, yes? There is the American dream, and here we have the Indonesian dream.".<ref name="NYT">{{cite news |title=Joko Widodo, Populist Governor, Is Named Winner in Indonesian Presidential Vote |author=Joe Cochrane |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2014/07/23/world/asia/joko-widodo-populist-governor-is-named-winner-in-indonesian-election.html?_r=0 |date=22 July 2014 |access-date=22 July 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140729004750/http://www.nytimes.com/2014/07/23/world/asia/joko-widodo-populist-governor-is-named-winner-in-indonesian-election.html?_r=1 |archive-date=29 July 2014 |url-status=dead}}</ref> Joko Widodo was the first Indonesian president to not be from the military or the political elite, and the political commentator Salim Said gave the popular view of the politician "someone who is our neighbour, who decided to get into politics and run for president".<ref name="NYT"/>

The Singaporean prime minister, [[Lee Hsien Loong]], posted his congratulations on Twitter minutes after the election, expressing hope that Joko Widodo would work towards improving [[Indonesia–Singapore relations|relations]] between the two countries.<ref name="Singapore">{{cite news |title=World leaders congratulate Jokowi |author=Bagus BT Saragih |work=The Jakarta Post |url=http://www.thejakartapost.com/news/2014/07/22/world-leaders-congratulate-jokowi.html |date=22 July 2014 |access-date=23 July 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140724171000/http://www.thejakartapost.com/news/2014/07/22/world-leaders-congratulate-jokowi.html |archive-date=24 July 2014 |url-status=dead}}</ref> [[Tony Abbott]], Prime Minister of Australia, stated that Joko Widodo's election was a "milestone" for the development of democracy in Indonesia, and stated his hope that the two countries' [[Australia–Indonesia relations|relations]] could be reinforced following a decline caused by espionage scandals and human trafficking.<ref name="Abbott">{{cite news |title=Australian PM congratulates Jokowi on Indonesia win |work=The Jakarta Post |url=http://www.thejakartapost.com/news/2014/07/23/australian-pm-congratulates-jokowi-indonesia-win.html |date=23 July 2014 |access-date=23 July 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140725182500/http://www.thejakartapost.com/news/2014/07/23/australian-pm-congratulates-jokowi-indonesia-win.html |archive-date=25 July 2014 |url-status=dead}}</ref> US President [[Barack Obama]] also congratulated Jokowi and is also willing to improve [[Indonesia-United States relations|relations]] between Indonesia and the US.<ref>{{cite news |title=Obama congratulates Indonesia's incoming president Jokowi |url=http://www.straitstimes.com/news/asia/south-east-asia/story/obama-congratulates-indonesias-incoming-president-jokowi-20140723 |access-date=20 October 2014 |work=Straits Times}}</ref> However, Prabowo asked for world leaders to withhold congratulatory statements to Jokowi.

====Appeal====
[[File:Voting bulletin of 2014 Indonesian presidential election.jpg|thumb|A voting ballot just after the official closing of elections at a voting station in Jakarta. The ballot is punched at section 1 (in favour of Prabowo Subianto)]]

A member of the Prabowo-Hatta campaign team outlined the eight final moves that Prabowo plans to take to overturn the election result. These are:<ref>{{cite web |author=Elvan Dany Sutrisno |title=8 Langkah Terakhir Prabowo (Prabowo's 8 Final Moves) |publisher=detik.com |date=25 July 2014 |url=http://news.detik.com/pemilu2014/read/2014/07/25/102017/2648002/1562/8-langkah-terakhir-prabowo |access-date=28 July 2014 |language=id |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140728091111/http://news.detik.com/pemilu2014/read/2014/07/25/102017/2648002/1562/8-langkah-terakhir-prabowo |archive-date=28 July 2014 |url-status=dead |df=dmy-all}}</ref>
# File a lawsuit over the election result with the Constitutional Court
# Report alleged ethical violations by the KPU to the Election Organisers Ethics Council (DKPP).
# File a report with the Election Supervisory Committee (Panwaslu).
# Report electoral violations to the police.
# Making a report to the Ombudsman.
# File a report with the State Administrative Court (PTUN) asking for the KPU on the election result to be annulled.
# Political manoeuvring within the [[People's Representative Council]] (DPR) by establishing a Presidential Election Special Committee to evaluate the performance of the KPU. The manoeuvring was done by parties within Prabowo-Hatta's coalition.
# A [[class action]].

Prabowo Subianto took an appeal against the election result to the Constitutional Court of Indonesia, alleging "structured, systematic and massive" violations and that up to 24.1 million votes were "troubled". The first hearing was on 6 August. Hundreds of supporters were present outside the court.<ref name=BBC_app1>{{cite news |title=Prabowo challenges Indonesia presidential election result |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/world-asia-28670712 |access-date=21 August 2014 |work=BBC |date=6 August 2014}}</ref> On 21 August the court delivered a unanimous 9–0 verdict in favour of rejecting all aspects of the appeal. A spokesperson for Subianto stated that his team did not consider the ruling fair, but they would accept the court's judgement.<ref name=BCC_app2>{{cite news |title=Subianto appeal denied as Indonesia court backs Widodo win |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/world-asia-28885781 |access-date=21 August 2014 |publisher=BBC |date=21 August 2014}}</ref> On the same day, the Election Organizers Ethics Council (DKPP) ruled that there had been some ethical violations. Of the nine local election commissioners dismissed for taking bribes, four of them took money from Prabowo's Gerindra Party.<ref>
{{cite web |title=In Prabowo's Complaint to Ethics Council, Verdict Finds Officials Took Bribes… From Gerindra |work=[[The Jakarta Globe]] |date=21 August 2014 |url=http://www.thejakartaglobe.com/news/prabowos-complaint-ethics-council-verdict-finds-officials-took-bribes-gerindra/#comment-39857 |access-date=22 August 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140823042435/http://www.thejakartaglobe.com/news/prabowos-complaint-ethics-council-verdict-finds-officials-took-bribes-gerindra/#comment-39857 |archive-date=23 August 2014 |url-status=dead |df=dmy-all}}
</ref>

